  "answer": 3,
  "factor": 3,
  "why": "Down to a few percent. At household scale that is the difference between selling a surplus and buying food back at post-harvest prices — the classic poverty trap, closed by a container costing a few dollars.",
  "context": "Roughly a quarter lost against a few percent — the cheapest calories are the ones already grown."
};

const agSecurityBranch = {
  "root": "A drought hits, and the exporting countries respond by restricting wheat and rice exports. World prices spike. Which leg of the food-security triad does this test, and who bears it?",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "text": "Availability, access, stability — the price crises of 2007–08 and 2021–22 broke one leg hardest. Which one, and for whom?",
      "choices": [
