};

const ag2050Cloze = {
  "prompt": "The honest 2050 arithmetic has a supply side and a demand side. File each lever:",
  "buckets": ["Supply side", "Demand side"],
  "items": [
    { "label": "closing yield gaps on smallholder farms", "bucket": 0 },
    { "label": "cutting the third of food that is never eaten", "bucket": 1 },
    { "label": "shifting diet composition toward plants", "bucket": 1 },
    { "label": "post-harvest storage and cold chains", "bucket": 0 }
  ],
  "why": "The FAO projection is demand growth of roughly half — about 50–60% over 2005/07 levels by 2050 — not a doubling, for roughly 10 billion people. Waste and diets are the demand-side levers, and yield-gap closing is the supply-side one."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/agriculture/expert/food-systems-and-security">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the agriculture tier's zoom-out, and it assumes the ladder below it. From the
    intermediate lessons you should carry <em>Soil health</em> and <em>Farm economics</em>;
    from the advanced tier, <em>Agroecology</em> and <em>Precision agriculture</em>. Those
    lessons taught what happens on the farm; this one follows the crop off it — through
    storage, trade and diet — and shows that hunger and waste are decided mostly past the
    field gate.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>trace the food chain end to end and locate its leverage points, on and off the farm;</li>
    <li>state the FAO's three-pillar definition of food security and use Sen's entitlement finding to analyse a famine;</li>
    <li>quantify post-harvest loss and explain why storage can out-produce seed technology per dollar;</li>
    <li>read the Green Revolution's ledger with both halves — the yields and the residues;</li>
    <li>evaluate the honest 2050 arithmetic: demand growth, yield gaps, waste and diets.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Food systems thinking is the agriculture ladder's zoom-out: <strong>farms are one
    stage in a chain whose outcomes — security, waste, emissions, prices — are system
    properties</strong>. The discipline measures the whole chain from input and land
    preparation through cultivation, processing, trade and consumption, and finds the
    leverage points are often not on the farm: enough calories are produced to feed
    everyone, while roughly a third of food is wasted and hundreds of millions remain
    undernourished. The syllabus's crop-production topics — land preparation and the
    cultivation of cash crops — are where the chain begins, and the expert reading
    takes them seriously as agronomy before asking what they cost and what they
    connect to.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the chain, waste, Sen's security triad, the 2050 question" widget="equationStep" config={foodSysCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the steps run from map to arithmetic. Step 1 draws the
    whole chain so "feed the world" stops meaning "grow more"; step 2 puts a number on the
    chain's biggest leak; step 3 redefines security so access and stability count as much as
    production; step 4 assembles the 2050 question those pieces set up. Keep asking of each
    later section: which step's problem is this?
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> outcomes like security and waste are properties of the chain, not
    the field — enough calories already exist, yet a third are lost and millions go hungry.
    The lesson now starts where the chain starts, in the agronomy, and works forward.
  </p>

  <h2>Land preparation: the agronomy, the labour, and what it spends</h2>
  <p>
    Land preparation (4.1) is the chain's first stage, and its methods encode a
    whole philosophy of soil. Conventional tillage — ploughing, harrowing, ridge
    formation — clears weeds, incorporates residues and creates a fine seedbed, at
    the cost of exposing soil organic matter to oxidation and leaving bare ground
    to rain: erosion's opening bid. The conservation-agriculture alternative —
    minimum or zero tillage, permanent ground cover, rotations — was built on
    evidence that undisturbed soil with residue cover can cut erosion by an order
    of magnitude while building organic carbon, with herbicides substituting for
    the plough's weed control. The economics decide adoption: no-till saves passes
    (fuel, labour, hours — decisive at scale, and the reason the US and southern
    Brazil converted tens of millions of hectares) but demands specific machinery
    and management skill, which is why adoption is uneven among smallholders.
    The worked example of the stakes: erosion rates under conventional practice
    typically exceed soil formation rates by factors of ten to a hundred —
    topsoil that takes centuries to form can wash away in a decade — which makes
    preparation method a genuine intergenerational decision, not a stylistic one.
    Land clearing itself (forest to cropland) is the other side of the same
    ledger: land-use change is a major emissions source and biodiversity loss
    driver, so "where should the next hectare come from?" is a system question
    (yield gap closing versus expansion) with global consequences.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="find the faulty line in this tillage ledger" widget="spot-error" config={agTillageSpot} />

  <h2>Cultivation of cash crops: specialisation, its logic and its hazards</h2>
  <p>
    Cash crops (4.2) — cotton, coffee, cocoa, tea, sugar, tobacco, groundnuts, cut
    flowers — are crops grown for sale rather than subsistence, and they carry the
    system's sharpest economics. The logic is real: comparative advantage and
    specialisation raise incomes; export crops earn the foreign exchange that
    pays for fertiliser and machinery; the Green Revolution's staples story
    (wheat and rice yields roughly doubling in Asia from the late 1960s, credited
    to Borlaug's semi-dwarf varieties, irrigation and fertiliser) shows what
    input packages can do. The hazards are equally structural: price volatility
    (commodity swings can halve a household's income year on year), market
    concentration (a few traders and buyers capturing value — cocoa farmers
    receive a small fraction of a chocolate bar's price), monoculture risk
    (pests and pathogens thrive in genetic uniformity — the 1970 Southern corn
    leaf blight in the US, a single cytoplasm's vulnerability costing 15% of
    the crop), and the food-first question: does export cultivation displace
    food? The evidence is mixed and case-specific — cash crops can coexist
    with food security (cotton revenues buying staples) or undermine it
    (colonial-style extraction; modern land acquisition). The design answers —
    farmer cooperatives, fair-trade and certification premiums, intercropping
    and diversification — are attempts to keep the income logic while damping
    the exposure.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="bet on the hazard of uniformity" widget="predict" config={agBlightPredict} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "cash crops are the cause of hunger — replace
    them with food crops and famine ends." Famine analysis demolished the simple
    version: Amartya Sen's entitlement work (Poverty and Famines, 1981) showed,
    case by case (the Bengal famine of 1943 among them), that famines occur
    with food <em>present</em> — people starve for lack of <em>access</em>:
    wages, prices, distribution and politics, not regional production totals.
    Cash crops are one variable in an access economy, sometimes the family's
    only income. The system view replaces the reflex with the harder
    question: who can buy, at what price, through what shock?
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="work the security triad" widget="branch" config={agSecurityBranch} />
  <Widget no="4" caption="true or false — the access economy" widget="judge" config={agSenJudge} />
  <p>
    The Green Revolution deserves its numbers read carefully, because it is the
    chain's biggest single success — and its cautionary tail. India's wheat
    harvest roughly doubled within a decade of the mid-1960s introduction of
    semi-dwarf, fertiliser-responsive varieties (from around 10–12 million
    tonnes to about 23 million by the early 1970s), and Borlaug's Nobel (1970)
    honoured a famine widely forecast and not delivered across South Asia. The
    varieties worked by converting fertiliser into grain rather than stalk —
    they were an input-package, not a seed alone: irrigation, credit and price
    policy were co-stars, which is why the revolution bypassed rain-fed regions
    and smallholders without them. The residue: groundwater depletion in Punjab,
    soil salinity on over-irrigated land, and a narrowed gene pool — the costs
    the sustainability agenda now services. Both halves of the ledger belong in
    any honest account of "produce more".
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the chain's first stages are genuine agronomy with genuine bills —
    tillage that trades soil for seedbeds, cash crops that trade exposure for income — and the
    production successes are real but conditional. Now the definition that decides whether any
    of it reaches eaters: security.
  </p>

  <h2>Security redefined: availability, access, stability</h2>
  <p>
    The FAO's definition makes the system explicit: food security exists when all
    people, at all times, have physical and economic access to sufficient, safe and
    nutritious food — availability (is it produced or importable?), access (can
    households afford it? the income-and-price side) and stability (do all of the
    above hold through droughts, price spikes and wars?). The 2007–08 and
    2021–22 price crises demonstrated the triad's third leg: droughts and export
    bans (Russia's wheat restrictions, India's rice limits) plus the Black Sea
    disruption sent world prices up sharply, and the import-dependent countries —
    not the food exporters — bore the hunger. The measurement discipline follows:
    dashboards now track grain stocks, import dependence, dietary diversity and
    real food prices the way finance tracks risk. Malnutrition adds the
    nutrition layer — micronutrient deficiency ("hidden hunger") coexists with
    calorie sufficiency, and obesity now coexists with underweight in the same
    countries — which is why "food security" and "nutrition security" are
    distinct design targets.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked waste calculation shows why the infrastructure lever dominates in
    poorer regions: a smallholder storing a tonne of grain in a traditional woven
    store can lose 20–30% to insects, rodents and moisture; a hermetic (airtight)
    bag or metal silo cuts that to a few percent. At household scale that is the
    difference between selling a surplus and buying food back at post-harvest
    prices — the classic poverty trap, closed by a container costing a few
    dollars. Multiply by millions of households and the storage intervention
    out-produces, per dollar, almost any seed technology — which is why
    development economists keep insisting that the food system's cheapest
    calories are the ones already grown.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="guess what a few dollars of container saves" widget="estimate" config={agStorageEstimate} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "eating local is the low-carbon choice — food
    miles dominate a meal's footprint." Transport is typically a small slice of
    food emissions — for most products well under a tenth of the total — while
    <em>production method</em> dominates: beef's footprint per kilogram is many
    times that of beans regardless of where either was grown, and season can beat
    distance (tomatoes grown locally in heated greenhouses can out-emit shipped
    field tomatoes). The honest rule: what you eat dwarfs where it came from,
    with transport as a tie-breaker between otherwise similar products.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="which curve shows what actually dominates a meal?" widget="trace" config={agFootprintTrace} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> security has three legs — availability, access, stability — and
    the price crises broke the third one for import-dependent countries, while the storage
    calculation showed access being built a container at a time. Next, the leverage points
    that need no new harvest at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Waste and demand: the leverage points off the farm</h2>
  <p>
    The chain's quiet scandal: roughly a third of food produced is never eaten.
    The geography splits the remedy: in poorer countries, losses are
    <em>post-harvest</em> — storage pests, spoilage for want of cold chains,
    transport (grain sheafs lost to rodents and moisture; dairy spoiling without
    refrigeration) — so the highest-return investments are unglamorous
    infrastructure: hermetic storage bags, rural roads, solar cold rooms. In
    richer countries, waste is <em>consumer and retail</em> — cosmetic standards,
    date-label confusion, portion oversizing, plate waste — so the levers are
    labelling, dynamic pricing, redistribution and norms. The demand side holds
    the biggest single lever: livestock products use the most land and water per
    calorie and protein (beef's footprint per kilogram exceeds beans' by more
    than an order of magnitude), so diet composition — the EAT-Lancet planetary
    health diet's shift toward plants — could free large areas of land and cut
    food-system emissions substantially, which is why the diet question, not
    only the yield question, dominates the 2050 arithmetic: roughly 10 billion
    people on a warming plateau, with yield gaps (smallholders often harvesting
    half or less of attainable yields) as the supply-side opportunity and waste
    and diets as the demand-side ones.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="pair each region's waste with its remedy" widget="match" config={agWasteMatch} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "we must double food production by 2050 or
    billions starve." The underlying FAO projection is demand growth of roughly
    half (about 50–60% over 2005/07 levels by 2050, with meat's share rising) —
    "doubling" is a repeated inflation of the real figure. More importantly, the
    demand that must be met is only loosely the production that exists: waste,
    feed-to-meat conversion losses and access failures mean the effective gap is
    smaller than the headline — which is precisely why the levers above
    (waste, diets, yield-gap closing) dominate the honest 2050 arithmetic.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="sort the 2050 levers into supply and demand" widget="sort-buckets" config={ag2050Cloze} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>State the three pillars of the FAO's food-security definition, and name the pillar on which Sen's famine analysis concentrates.</li>
    <li>A smallholder stores a tonne of grain in a woven store, losing about a quarter of it; a hermetic bag cuts losses to a few percent. Estimate the grain saved, then explain why development economists rank storage above new seed varieties per dollar.</li>
    <li>A friend switches to locally grown, heated-greenhouse tomatoes to cut their food footprint. Using this lesson, what is wrong with the reasoning?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Availability, access, stability — and Sen's entitlement
    work shows famines are overwhelmingly <em>access</em> failures: food present in the
    region, people unable to buy it (Bengal, 1943). (2) Roughly 220–270 kg saved per tonne
    (from about 25% losses down to a few percent); the container costs a few dollars and
    repeats every season, while the intervention needs no agronomic change, no credit and no
    extension advice — which is why the cheapest calories are the ones already grown.
    (3) Transport is typically a small slice of food emissions, while production method
    dominates: heated greenhouse tomatoes can out-emit shipped field tomatoes. What you eat
    dwarfs where it came from; distance is only a tie-breaker.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Food outcomes are system properties — leverage often sits off the farm.</li>
    <li>Security = availability + access + stability; famines are access failures, not absolute shortages.</li>
    <li>A third of food is never eaten: post-harvest losses in poorer countries, consumer-retail waste in richer ones.</li>
    <li>The Green Revolution was an input package, not a seed — real gains, real residues.</li>
    <li>Diet composition, not food miles, is the biggest single demand-side lever.</li>
    <li>The honest 2050 arithmetic: about 50–60% demand growth, met by yield-gap closing, waste cuts and diet shifts.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The supply side of the same arithmetic is the sibling expert lesson,
    <em>Climate-smart agriculture</em> — how the production half of the chain adapts to a
    warming plateau while cutting its own emissions, which is the other half of the 2050
    question this page could only frame.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Yield gaps: the quietest opportunity, quantified.</strong> Global yield
    gap atlases compare actual against attainable (water- and nutrient-limited)
    yields: the gaps are enormous in sub-Saharan Africa (often a third of
    attainable or less) and modest in the high-input breadbaskets. Closing even
    half the gap through agronomy, inputs, storage, credit and roads — the
    unglamorous package — would out-produce most headline technologies, without
    clearing another hectare. The counterweight is sustainability: yield-gap
    closing done input-heavy recreates the eutrophication and depletion problems
    (the Ogallala aquifer, the Gulf of Mexico dead zone) elsewhere, so the
    research frontier is <em>sustainable intensification</em>: more output per
    unit of land, water and nutrient — precision agriculture, improved
    varieties, integrated soil fertility management — with randomised-trial
    evidence (the development field's toolkit) increasingly available on which
    interventions actually move smallholder yields.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Trade as buffer and transmitter.</strong> The system's stability
    architecture is trade: it moves surpluses to deficits and (in principle)
    dampens local shocks — but it transmits them too, and its failure modes are
    political. 2008's price spike deepened when major exporters banned exports
    (each ban rational domestically, compounding globally); 2022's Black Sea
    disruption showed a single corridor's leverage over world wheat. The design
    responses sit between self-sufficiency fantasies (costly everywhere,
    impossible in much of the Middle East and Africa) and pure openness
    (strategically fragile): regional reserves (ASEAN's rice pool), humanitarian
    stocks, transparency mechanisms (the Agricultural Market Information System
    built after 2008), and disciplining export bans. Food is geopolitics with
    calories attached — the system view's least avoidable conclusion.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The frontier: measuring the whole chain, and pricing the
    externalities.</strong> Life-cycle assessment now attaches emissions, water
    and land footprints to specific foods (the data behind the diet lever);
    satellite systems verify deforestation-free supply chains (the EU's
    deforestation regulation makes it law for imports); traceability pilots run
    cocoa and coffee from plot to bar; and true-cost accounting attempts prices
    that include health and environmental externalities — the estimates are
    large (the UN's Food System Economics Commission puts the hidden costs in
    the trillions of dollars annually) and the accounting contested, but the
    direction of travel is clear: a food system that was priced as if soil,
    water, climate and health were free is being slowly repriced. The
    agricultural ladder's opening topics — preparing land, planting a crop —
    end this page as policy variables, which is exactly what "food system"
    means.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Sen, Poverty and Famines (1981); FAO SOFI reports; EAT-Lancet Commission (2019); the waste and development lessons. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
